  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO\nCOUNTIES...\n\n\n...UPDATED AVIATION DISCUSSION...\n\nThe 00z sounding Friday afternoon shows a well mixed marine boundary \nlayer, around 3000 feet deep. Low clouds will be patchy overnight \nthrough the coastal basin and scatter out to the coast between 8-11 \nAM this morning.\n\nWhile the coastal basin will maintain modest minimum relative \nhumidity values (30-60% inland, 60-80% coast) this afternoon, \nconditions will be very dry for higher mountain communities and \neastward into the deserts, dropping to 8-15%. These low daytime \nhumidities improve for mountains/deserts through Monday afternoon \nwith more typical minimum RH values down to 15-30%.\n\nThe closed upper low over Idaho continues to trek eastward and is \nbeing replaced by a shortwave ridge, bumping up our temperatures \nthis afternoon by 3-6 degrees relative to yesterday. The ridge moves \nout of the region quickly as another closed low approaches from the \nwest Sunday, centering over Central California by Monday. \nTemperatures once again drop to 6-12 degrees below average across \ninland areas Monday as the marine layer deepens and low clouds fill \nthe coastal basin during the nights and early mornings of early next \nweek.\n\nThe upper closed low weakens and combines with the main jet stream \nin the Northern Rockies, leaving behind very weak higher pressure \nanomalies over the eastern Great Basin and thus perhaps a very \nweak N/E low level offshore pressure gradient Wednesday (~20% \nchance). The extent of the effects would be limited to how dry the\nair would be through the deserts and mountains and thus how much \nthe daytime temperatures could spike for inland later in the week.\nWith the trough already moving out, temperatures are already \nforecast to jump up 5-10 degrees into Wednesday with highs later \nin the week (including Wednesday) reaching back into the low-100s \nin the low deserts, mid-90s in the IE, mid/upper 80s inland and \naround 80 for coastal areas. These are all temperatures typical \nfor mid-September, though maybe a few degrees above average at the\ncoast.\n\nBeyond, WPC model clusters are converging on solutions showing\nanother trough developing off of the west coast by next weekend. \nThe amplitude and intensity of this trough are uncertain, and \nthere is thus uncertainty about whether temperatures stay near \naverage or dip below average again.",
